 hidden by this  gravitational pole effect which we are  able to expose but the cost of exposing  it was one correct prediction out of  every thousand and so the data science  team was able to calculate this we call  this the purrito curve and we showed it  the four score leadership and we  explained them what the two metrics were  because I think they're fairly easy to  understand and four score leadership  picked a point on this curve which they  thought was the most the best value for  the business and that is what we have in  production today and so to summarize I  want to emphasize the three the three  lessons which we learned or to keep make  sure you're keeping track of the right  metrics don't undervalue don't  underestimate the value of engineering  your features and make sure you know  your data no matter how good you think  it is there's usually something you can  learn just by rolling up your sleeves  and diving in there for for some time  and so before I end I went to credit  where credit's due I didn't do all this  work myself this is actually done by a  talented team of machine learning  machine learning engineers and servant  engineers and data scientists at  Foursquare and like every other company  at this conference we are also hiring  we're looking for data scientists data  engineers managers and so forth and so  if you're interested come talk to me I  have four co-workers who are in the  audience they will be at my office hours  that office will be at coffee so you can  talk to us and that's the end of my talk  thank you very much  [Applause]  locations and popularity so the question  is how different is our place data from  Google Maps they do and they are and  they are a competitor to some parts of  Foursquare technology I can't speak for  the places database I can speak for the  the accuracy of the predictions because  we have side-by-side studies with many  of our competitors and we probably  shouldn't do this but we have some apps  are for an SDK side-by-side with the  competitors of our SDK and we are at  least 10 points 10 percentage points  more accurate than others so primarily  because we have so much labeled data  yeah yeah  yes the question was if there is use  case for ephemeral events the answer is  yes though I can't think of an example  off the top of my head we are able to  detect those and often we know about  them in advance and so if you use a  swarm app a lot of times when you check  in if we think that there is a specific  event happening we actually write before  you create the check-in we ask you to  confirm that you're at that specific  event so we do that for a lot for movie  openings we do that for Mathers and  score eats like I said and so we have  that data and it's in the real time  database that we can pull at any time as  far as a use cases downstream I I can't  answer that but I'm fairly certain yes  there is an application for it but I  don't think it's any of the ones that  when I was showing you the picture of  the brands that we have is actually not  one of the things that we do right now  so  [Music]

